// The cdz-kernel AUTHORIZER world — the guest boundary for a policy component that decides whether a
// requested effect is permitted (§10 / SEC-F1), as a wasm component-model interface. Operator ruling:
// authorization is a Cedar policy set compiled to a wasm COMPONENT, hosted by the SAME wasmtime
// component infra as the reducer (wasm_host.rs) — NOT an in-Rust interim. This .wit IS the host↔policy
// contract; a `ComponentAuthorizer` binds it via `bindgen!` and impls the kernel `Authorize` trait, so a
// policy component drops into the kernel wherever the flat-capability `Authorizer` does.
//
// ── Why a SEPARATE world from the reducer ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// A policy component EXPORTS `authorize(request) -> decision` and IMPORTS NOTHING (no kv — a policy is a
// PURE function of the request + its embedded policy set; §10 authorize contract: total + pure + no
// I/O). That's a different shape from the reducer world (fold.apply + kv import), so it needs its own
// small world. The generic component HOSTING (Engine/Component/Linker/instantiate) is shared with the
// reducer path; only the world type differs.
//
// ── The request: a PARC-style triple (principal, action, resource) ───────────────────────────────
// Mirrors Cedar's authorization model (verified feasible by v-agent-harness-host: cedar-policy 4.x
// compiles for wasm32 and expresses deny-by-default + forbid-overrides-permit). The kernel maps an
// `EffectRequest` to this triple: `action` = the effect FAMILY string (the seq-39 source of truth —
// "http"/"model"/"shell"/… for the well-known kinds, AND register-by-string families like "store/set",
// whose EffectKind is a placeholder; the flat `Authorizer` gates on this same family, so the policy sees
// the same action string), `resource` = the resolved TARGET (the SEC-F1 security-relevant string the
// policy gates — a URL, model id, command, or store NAME), `principal` = who is acting (the session/agent
// identity; v0 passes the session id).
// `payload` is NOT part of the authz decision (SEC-F1 gates the kind+target, not the body).
//
// ── Determinism ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The decision must be a PURE function of (request, embedded policy) — no clock, no I/O, no ambient
// state — so an authz decision is replay-stable (the same request yields the same decision, and the
// kernel logs it in the AuthzDenied event either way, §10). The wasm sandbox + the no-imports world
// enforce the absence of ambient I/O.
//
// ⚠ CONTRACT STABILITY: like reducer.wit + cadenza:runtime, the SHAPE below is the host↔policy contract.
// Additive evolution only (new optional fields appended) — never reorder/remove. DRAFT for review: the
// exact request/decision fields are being confirmed with v-agent-harness-host (who builds the Cedar
// policy guest against this shape) before the first policy component binds it.
package cadenza:agent-kernel-authz;

interface types {
  // A requested effect the policy decides on — the PARC triple (principal, action, resource). The
  // kernel derives it from an `EffectRequest`: action=kind, resource=target, principal=session identity.
  record auth-request {
    // Who is acting — the session/agent identity (v0: the session id string). Cedar's PRINCIPAL.
    principal: string,
    // What verb — the effect FAMILY string ("http", "model", "shell", …, and register-by-string families
    // like "store/set"/"store/resolve"). Cedar's ACTION. This is the content-type family (seq-39 source of
    // truth), NOT the EffectKind enum: a store/* or extension family carries a placeholder kind, so keying
    // on the enum would misreport it as "emit" — the family string is what the flat Authorizer gates on too.
    action: string,
    // The resolved target the policy gates (URL / model id / command / session id). Cedar's RESOURCE.
    // This is the SEC-F1 security-relevant string — a policy admits/denies on principal×action×target.
    // (Named `target`, not `resource`: `resource` is a reserved WIT keyword — component-model resources.)
    target: string,
    // The STRUCTURED SUBJECT of a subject-scoped effect — a SECOND resource dimension, NOT opaque body.
    // For a group membership op (`store/add`/`store/remove`) the `target` is the GROUP NAME and this is the
    // MEMBER VALUE (a SessionId hex); it lets a policy express the §directory-D5 self-vs-other rule
    // (add/remove SELF where `subject == principal` is allowed by the join grant; add/remove OTHER requires
    // the group's owner authority). `none` for effects with no structured subject (http/model/shell/…, and
    // `store/set`/`store/resolve`, whose security-relevant string is fully captured by `target`). This does
    // NOT admit general effect BODY into the authz decision — SEC-F1 still gates on (principal, action,
    // target); `subject` is a typed, effect-derived identity the kernel extracts (e.g. by decoding a
    // member-op payload), not arbitrary caller bytes. Additive + optional, so existing policies ignore it.
    subject: option<string>,
  }

  // The policy's verdict. `allow` = permit the effect (proceed to dispatch); otherwise deny, carrying a
  // human/diagnostic reason recorded in the AuthzDenied event (§10). `reason` is also meaningful on an
  // allow (an audit note), though the kernel only surfaces it on a denial. Additive room for Cedar
  // obligations/advice later (a future optional field), not modeled in v0.
  record decision {
    // True = permit; false = deny.
    allow: bool,
    // Why (a denial reason for the audit log, or an allow note). Empty string = no reason given.
    reason: string,
  }
}

// ── The authorize export — the policy component's one entrypoint ─────────────────────────────────
interface authorizer {
  use types.{auth-request, decision};

  // Decide whether `request` is permitted. PURE + total (§10 / §17 can't-brick): it inspects the request
  // against the policy set embedded in the component and returns a decision; it must not trap. A trapped
  // authorize is treated by the host as a fail-CLOSED deny (a policy that can't decide denies — the safe
  // default), never a panic.
  authorize: func(request: auth-request) -> decision;
}

// ── The authorizer world: what a policy component EXPORTS + IMPORTS ──────────────────────────────
world authorizer-world {
  // A policy imports NOTHING — it's a pure function of the request + its embedded policy set (no kv, no
  // host functions). This is what makes an authz decision deterministic + sandboxed.
  export authorizer;
}
